  "modelDocument": "| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Document | BCN-R1-01 Programme brief |\n| Owner | Programme Manager |\n| Approver | Dr. Helen Marchetti (E-001), VP Clinical Operations, executive sponsor |\n| Version | 1.1 (1.0 issued at kick-off on 2026-09-01) |\n| Date | 2026-10-01 |\n| Status | Approved. Figures are the Q3 2026 baseline (1 July to 30 September 2026) as of 2026-10-01 |\n\n## The problem in numbers\n\nKestrel Health Network (KHN) runs Kestrel General Hospital (KGH), Aspen Ridge Hospital (ARH), Coulee Valley Hospital (CVH) and Prairie Gate Health Centre (PGH). Three problems put patients and their information at risk.\n\n1. Patient-flow leaders work from Excel extracts emailed every morning. Sites count differently, nobody trusts the numbers, and the extracts carry more patient detail than anyone needs. At KGH the median time from arrival to physician initial assessment (PIA) was 80.5 minutes, and 7.5% of emergency department (ED) visits left without being seen (LWBS), against 5.7% across all sites.\n2. Clinicians wait days for access and some people keep it after they leave. In the service desk extract (August and September 2026) the median access request took 4.26 days and only 27.3% were resolved within 72 hours. Two workers who had left still had enabled accounts, 2 of 6 privileged accounts had no multi-factor authentication (MFA), and one administrator held provision, approve and audit-log rights on the Identity Directory.\n3. Kestrel cannot show it could keep caring for patients through a ransomware attack. Both restore tests of the Clinical Information System (CIS, A-001) missed its 4-hour recovery time objective (RTO), at 9.0 and 13.5 hours. The last test was on 2025-08-14, 413 days before 2026-10-01.\n\n## What Beacon delivers\n\nOne programme, one charter, one budget, one schedule and one change-control board (CCB), from 2026-09-01 to 2026-12-17.\n\n| Release | What it delivers | Gate |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Patient-flow analytics service | Power BI dashboards for ED flow and bed occupancy on de-identified, aggregated data, secured by site with row-level security (RLS), supported by the service desk | Go-live 2026-10-23 |\n| 2 Clinician identity and access | Access driven by human resources (HR) joiner-mover-leaver events and roles, MFA and single sign-on, privileged access control, quarterly access reviews, access requests as a measured service | Acceptance 2026-11-26 |\n| 3 Cyber resilience | Business impact analysis, recovery targets, a tested CIS restore and downtime procedures, incident response, vulnerability remediation, a security-operations dashboard, programme closure | Programme acceptance 2026-12-17 |\n\n## Who is involved\n\n- Dr. Helen Marchetti (E-001), executive sponsor: owns the business case, holds the management reserve, decides the gates.\n- Marcus Bell (E-002), Chief Information Officer: chairs the CCB.\n- Priya Raman (E-003), Chief Privacy Officer: approves the privacy impact assessment.\n- Daniel Osei (E-004), Chief Information Security Officer: security owner and risk acceptance advisor.\n- Grace Liu (E-005), IT Service Desk Manager: service owner of the analytics service, which her service desk supports.\n- Tomás Rivera (E-006), Clinical Analytics Lead: builds the semantic model, data steward.\n- Dr. Sarah Whitford (E-007) owns emergency data. Janelle Cardinal (E-008) owns bed data and is the main dashboard user.\n- Programme Manager: runs the plan, the RAID (risks, assumptions, issues and decisions) log and reporting.\n\n## How success is measured\n\nBaselines are Kestrel data. Targets are agreed with the sponsor and measured three months after each gate: Release 1 measures on 2027-01-25, Release 2 measures on 2027-02-26, and Release 3 and programme measures on 2027-03-17.\n\n| Measure | Baseline (data) | Target (agreed) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Median minutes, arrival to PIA, KGH | 80.5 | 60 |\n| LWBS rate, all sites | 5.7% | 4.5% |\n| Access requests resolved within 72 hours | 27.3% | 80% |\n| Median days to fulfil an access request | 4.26 | 1 |\n| Missed leavers (accounts enabled after the worker left) | 2 | 0 |\n| Privileged accounts with MFA | 66.7% | 100% |\n| Q3 access reviews completed on time | 66.7% | 100% |\n| CIS restore within its 4-hour RTO | Failed (9.0 h and 13.5 h) | Pass |\n| Security awareness, agency and vendor workers | 48% | 90% |\n\n## Budget and timeline\n\n- Budget at completion (BAC): CAD (Canadian dollars) 1,200,000 across 15 work packages. Release 1 CAD 420,000, Release 2 CAD 460,000, Release 3 CAD 320,000.\n- Management reserve (agreed, held by the sponsor): CAD 120,000. Total authorized: CAD 1,320,000.\n- 78 working days, Monday to Friday. Gates on 2026-10-23, 2026-11-26 and 2026-12-17.\n- Position on 2026-10-01: earned value (EV) CAD 224,000 against planned value (PV) CAD 278,900 and actual cost (AC) CAD 250,600. Cost performance index (CPI) 0.89 and schedule performance index (SPI) 0.80: behind plan and over cost.\n\n## Top risks and open issues\n\n- R-01 ED extract data quality undermines trust in the dashboards. Owner: Tomás Rivera.\n- R-02 Small counts at PGH (55 valid visits in Q3) could identify patients. Owner: Priya Raman.\n- R-04 The CIS cannot be restored within its 4-hour RTO. Owner: Daniel Osei.\n- R-08 Cost performance could exhaust the management reserve. Owner: Marcus Bell.\n- Open issues: I-01 Kevin Park (E-009) holds provision, approve and audit-log rights; I-02 missed leavers E-014 and E-027 still enabled; I-03 CIS restore tests failed twice.\n\nAll eight risks (R-01 to R-08) are in the RAID log, BCN-R1-11.\n\n## How Beacon is run\n\n- Every decision gets an ID in the RAID log. D-01: charter approved by Dr. Helen Marchetti on 2026-09-01.\n- Changes to scope, gate dates or budget go to the CCB before any baseline moves.\n- Dashboards are classified Confidential. Kestrel policy suppresses any count under 5, and blanks any rate whose numerator is under 5, in any report shared outside the care team.\n- The sponsor gets a weekly status report. Documents are numbered BCN-R1, BCN-R2 and BCN-R3 by release.",
